\[cos\theta^{*} = \sqrt{1-\frac{4p_{T}^{2}}{M_{W}^{2}}}, \tag{4.4}\] \[\frac{dcos\theta^{*}}{dp_{T}^{2}} = \frac{2}{M_{W}^{2}cos\theta^{*}},\] (4.5) \[\frac{d\sigma}{dp_{T}^{2}}=\frac{d\sigma}{dcos\theta^{*}} \times \frac{dcos\theta^{*}}{dp_{T}^{2}}=\frac{d\sigma}{dcos\theta^{*}} \times\frac{2}{M_{W}^{2}\sqrt{1-\frac{4p_{T}^{2}}{M_{W}^{2}}}} \tag{4.6}\]

where the cross section has a singularity at \(p_{T}=M_{W}/2\). This is the so-called "Jacobian peak" characterizing two body decays. This is under the assumption that \(W\) has no transverse momentum (\(p_{T}^{W}=0\)) and zero width (\(\Gamma_{W}=0\)) for an ideal case. In practice, the \(p_{T}\) distribution would be smeared to a peak with finite maximum and width. Figure 4.11 shows the Leading Order (LO) and Next-to-Leading Order (NLO) theoretical predictions for lepton \(p_{T}\) distributions in \(W\) production.

